@@ -133,6 +135,123 @@ See [Advanced and testing options](#advanced-and-testing-options) for a small se
 of keys that exist for development and benchmarking and should not be used on a
 production server.
 
+#### NFSv4 READ events
+
+When `nfs4_read_correlation` is enabled, Chimera attempts one INFO event with
+`schema=nfs4_read_v2` for each first-execution NFSv4.1 session `READ` reaching
+its decision layer. NFSv4.0, replayed cached compounds, and READs not reached
+because an earlier operation failed do not emit. The record includes a random
+process-local `local_id`, producer epoch/sequence, request decision
+`event_time_ns`, worker serialization `sink_time_ns`, target creation time,
+backing `volume_id`, canonical SHA-256 `file_id`, base64url export name, a
+base64url wrapped NFS `target_fh`, hashed client/session evidence, deterministic
+operation ID, compound operation/slot/sequence indexes, bounded compound tag
+plus digest, requested range, returned bytes, NFS status, outcome, EOF, and
+optional post-read file size.
+
+`file_id` is SHA-256 over
+`"chimera-read-v2\0" || mount_id[16] || BE16(inner_fh_len) || inner_fh`; export
+aliases and re-wrapping under a rotated NFS FH key therefore retain one model
+identity. `operation_id` is SHA-256 over
+`"chimera-read-op-v2\0" || sessionid[16] || BE32(slotid) || BE32(sequenceid) ||
+BE32(compound_op_index)`, making multi-READ compounds distinct while cached
+replay remains silent. Raw client and session identifiers are not logged.
+
+`sink_time_ns` is an availability timestamp for normalizer `ingest_time` /
+`known_at`; `event_time_ns` remains the request event time. Normally sink time
+is later, but wall-clock adjustment means consumers must not require a strict
+ordering between the two.
+
+`target_fh` is an executable opaque locator: a suitably authorized private
+warmer can use it with `PUTFH` followed by `OPEN(CLAIM_FH)` without resolving a
+mutable path. It does not bypass normal NFS credentials, export policy, ACL, or
+share checks, but it exposes stable file identity and must therefore be treated
+as sensitive capability-like data. This MVP writes it to the ordinary INFO
+log. That is a deliberate non-production privacy boundary: restrict log access,
+retention, and export before enabling the stream. No file data is emitted.
+
+The raw compound tag is copied into the event with a 256-byte bound, base64url
+encoded for logging, and accompanied by its original length, truncation flag,
+and SHA-256 digest. Structured tags have the exact 104-byte ASCII form
+`pc1:<role>:<plan_ref32hex>:<task_ref32hex>:<nonce32hex>`, where role is `w`
+(warmer), `d` (controlled demand), or `s` (synthetic). Such roles are reported
+as `origin_claim` with `origin_trust=client_asserted`; invalid/unstructured
+tags report `origin_claim=unknown origin_trust=client_asserted`. Chimera never labels an
+ordinary READ as trusted foreground demand.
+
+For deterministic training-data retrieval, set `nfs4_read_event_path` to a
+dedicated file on an owner-only persistent mount. The worker appends one bounded
+JSON object per line. It opens nonblocking with `O_NOFOLLOW`, accepts only a
+regular file owned by the daemon user with one link, and only then forces mode
+`0600`; FIFOs, sockets, devices, directories, symlinks, wrong-owner files, and
+empty/overlong paths are rejected. It calls `fdatasync` before publishing the
+event's synced watermark. On a partial write or sync failure it truncates back
+to the pre-record offset so a later retry cannot leave malformed JSONL. It
+drains the queue and flushes the sink during normal teardown. The file rotates
+at 64 MiB to a single `.1` backup using directory-relative operations and
+directory fsyncs, bounding each pod's sink footprint to approximately 128 MiB.
+File I/O is performed only by the event worker; NFS request threads retain the
+same bounded trylock enqueue and never wait for storage. If the file cannot be
+opened or written, the worker counts a `stage="sink"` drop and retries opening
+it on the next event.
+
+The ordinary INFO line is diagnostic and may still be rejected by the shared
+nonblocking logger; it is not the authoritative training transport. An empty
+compound tag is valid and is rendered as an empty `compound_tag_b64` value.
+
+The event records an operation decision before compound serialization, not
+wire delivery or client receipt; a later compound-level error may replace the
+response. It deliberately identifies a pseudonymous backing volume and file,
+and includes an executable wrapped target FH, but does not report CUFS cache
+state. Treat the stream as sensitive access-pattern and object-identity data:
+limit it to the PoC owner/dispatcher, use the shortest practical retention,
+prevent broad log export, and securely delete it after the experiment.
+Re-executed retransmissions have distinct event IDs, while cached NFS replays
+and READs not reached because an earlier compound operation failed have no
+event. NFSv3, SMB, S3, `READ_PLUS`, and internal reads are not covered.
+
+The stream is best effort, not an audit log. Queue admission, encoding, sink
+I/O, rollback, rotation, and diagnostic logging may fail. Process-local
+`chimera_nfs4_read_events_dropped{stage="queue"}` counts failures to timestamp
+or enqueue, including queue contention and saturation; `stage="encode"` counts
+invalid bounded event data; `stage="sink"` counts JSONL open/write/sync errors;
+and `stage="log"` counts diagnostic logger admission failures, including
+disabled or below-INFO logging. `stage="rollback"` counts a failure to truncate
+a partial record; `stage="rotation"` counts a failure in the durable rotation
+sequence. The worker publishes counter updates
+periodically; counters reset on restart. A successful JSONL line with zero
+queue/encode/sink drops is the delivery acceptance criterion; `stage="log"`
+does not affect authoritative sink delivery. Events are independent of sampled
+tracing.
+
+`chimera_nfs4_read_event_state{state=...}` exposes:
+
+- `last_enqueued_sequence`: newest event admitted to the in-process queue;
+- `last_synced_sequence`: newest producer sequence whose JSONL record completed
+  `fdatasync`;
+- `synced_count`: total records synced by this process;
+- `queue_depth`: events still waiting in the worker queue;
+- `worker_in_flight`: one while the worker is encoding/writing/syncing an event;
+- `delivery_clean`: epoch-scoped latch initialized to one and permanently set
+  to zero by any queue/encode/sink/rollback/rotation drop;
+- `barrier_ready`: coherent worker snapshot equal to one only when
+  `last_enqueued_sequence == last_synced_sequence`, queue depth is zero, and no
+  event is in flight. A publisher rechecks `delivery_clean` after publishing
+  candidate readiness, so a concurrent foreground drop cannot restore ready.
+
+`chimera_nfs4_read_event_producer_info{producer_epoch="<uuid>"} 1` exposes the
+exact, lossless producer epoch carried by JSONL records. The epoch is created
+once per NFS event-service initialization and remains immutable until teardown;
+an NFS service reinitialization in the same pod creates a new epoch. Collectors
+must reject a JSONL record whose epoch does not match the live producer info
+metric and must never subtract counters across epochs.
+
+A line becoming visible is not durability proof. Consumers must wait until the
+line's `producer_sequence` is at most `last_synced_sequence`, `queue_depth` is
+zero, `worker_in_flight` is zero, `delivery_clean` and `barrier_ready` are one,
+and
+queue/encode/sink/rollback/rotation drops remain zero.
+
 #### `server.smb_auth`
 
 Domain-authentication backends for SMB.
diff --git a/src/common/logging.c b/src/common/logging.c
index 8f287e75c0c2b9c0378aac597474ac1144c9ec6a..4d9b372ff56b43c755dc39100cfe2b6422fce448 100644
--- a/src/common/logging.c
+++ b/src/common/logging.c
@@ -390,6 +390,64 @@ chimera_vlog(
     pthread_mutex_unlock(&ChimeraLogBufLock);
 } /* chimera_vlog */
 
+SYMBOL_EXPORT int
+chimera_log_try_info(
+    const char *mod,
+    const char *file,
+    int         line,
+    const char *fmt,
+    ...)
+{
+    char            message[2048];
+    char            entry[3072];
+    struct timespec ts;
+    struct tm       tm_info;
+    va_list         args;
+    int             n, length;
+
+    if (ChimeraLogDisabled || ChimeraLogLevel < CHIMERA_LOG_INFO) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+
+    va_start(args, fmt);
+    n = vsnprintf(message, sizeof(message), fmt, args);
+    va_end(args);
+    if (n < 0 || (size_t) n >= sizeof(message)) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+
+    if (clock_gettime(CLOCK_REALTIME, &ts) != 0) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    chimera_timet2tmZ(ts.tv_sec, &tm_info);
+    length = snprintf(entry, sizeof(entry),
+                      "time=%04d-%02d-%02dT%02d:%02d:%02d.%09ldZ message=\"%s\""
+                      " process=%" PRIu64 " thread=%" PRIu64
+                      " level=info module=%s source=\"%s:%d\"\n",
+                      tm_info.tm_year + 1900, tm_info.tm_mon + 1,
+                      tm_info.tm_mday, tm_info.tm_hour, tm_info.tm_min,
+                      tm_info.tm_sec, ts.tv_nsec, message,
+                      (uint64_t) getpid(), (uint64_t) gettid(),
+                      mod, file, line);
+    if (length < 0 || (size_t) length >= sizeof(entry)) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    if (pthread_mutex_trylock(&ChimeraLogBufLock) != 0) {
+        return 0;
+    }
+    if (!ChimeraLogBuf ||
+        (size_t) (ChimeraLogBuf + CHIMERA_LOG_BUF_SIZE - ChimeraLogBufPtr) <=
+        (size_t) length) {
+        pthread_mutex_unlock(&ChimeraLogBufLock);
+        return 0;
+    }
+
+    memcpy(ChimeraLogBufPtr, entry, length + 1);
+    ChimeraLogBufPtr += length;
+    pthread_mutex_unlock(&ChimeraLogBufLock);
+    return 1;
+} /* chimera_log_try_info */
+
 SYMBOL_EXPORT void
 __chimera_debug(
     const char *mod,
